DATA=[{'filename': 'como-cuidar-muebles-de-madera-en-casa.html', 'title': 'How to Care for Wooden Furniture and Keep It in Good Condition', 'category': 'HOME DECOR', 'meta': 'Learn how to care for wooden furniture with gentle cleaning, moisture control, sun and heat protection, finish-specific maintenance, and preventive inspections.', 'intro': 'Wooden furniture can remain beautiful for many years when cleaning, moisture protection, heat control, and maintenance are adapted to the finish and daily use of each piece.', 'body': "\n<h2>In This Article</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Introduction</li>\n<li>Clean Wood Correctly Without Damaging It</li>\n<li>Control Humidity and Spills</li>\n<li>Protect Furniture From Sunlight and Heat</li>\n<li>Maintain Furniture According to Its Finish</li>\n<li>Create a Practical Long-Term Care Routine</li>\n<li>Common Mistakes to Avoid</li>\n<li>Special Care According to How the Furniture Is Used</li>\n<li>Frequently Asked Questions</li>\n<li>Conclusion</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>Introduction</h2>\n<p>Wooden furniture can remain attractive for many years when cleaning, moisture control, heat protection, and periodic maintenance are adapted to the finish and the way each piece is used. The most important rule is to identify the surface before applying any product. A varnished table, an oiled cabinet, a waxed chest, and a lacquered shelf do not necessarily require the same treatment.</p>\n<p>Preventive care is usually safer and easier than trying to repair heavy damage later. Small habits such as removing spills promptly, protecting surfaces from hot objects, keeping furniture away from persistent dampness, and checking joints periodically can extend the life of a piece without turning maintenance into a complicated routine.</p>\n\n<h2>Clean Wood Correctly Without Damaging It</h2>\n<p>Daily cleaning should be gentle. Dust can look harmless, but mixed with moisture or grease it can become a film that is harder to remove. For many sealed surfaces, a clean, soft, slightly damp cloth is enough, followed by drying. Before using any cleaner, identify whether the furniture is varnished, waxed, oiled, lacquered, painted, or finished in another way.</p>\n<p>Avoid soaking the wood. Excess water can enter joints, edges, cracks, or areas where the finish is worn. Unless the manufacturer says otherwise, it is usually safer to apply a cleaning product to the cloth rather than pouring or spraying it directly onto the furniture.</p>\n<p>For localized dirt, work gradually. Abrasive pads, harsh scrubbing, and aggressive household chemicals can dull or damage finishes. Start with the least aggressive method and test any unfamiliar product in a hidden area.</p>\n<p>Tables and other frequently used surfaces benefit from having crumbs, grease, and spills removed soon after use. Pay attention to corners, handles, drawer pulls, and joints where dirt tends to collect. If a piece is antique, valuable, or has an unknown finish, professional restoration advice may be safer than experimenting.</p>\n\n<h2>Control Humidity and Spills</h2>\n<p>Wood responds to changes in environmental humidity. It can expand, contract, warp, or develop movement at joints. For that reason, indoor wooden furniture should not remain against damp walls, near leaks, or in areas exposed to constant steam.</p>\n<p>Kitchens and bathrooms can require extra attention because humidity changes quickly. Good ventilation and prompt drying of splashes are important. If the piece is not designed for a humid environment, moving it to a more stable location may be better than repeatedly trying to treat the surface.</p>\n<p>When water, coffee, or another liquid is spilled, blot it promptly with an absorbent cloth instead of spreading it over a larger area. Coasters, placemats, and trivets reduce rings, heat marks, and discoloration. Plant pots also need protective saucers or trays so moisture does not remain trapped against wood for hours.</p>\n<p>Very dry conditions can also be a problem for some solid-wood furniture. The goal is not a perfect humidity number but avoiding extreme and sudden changes. Keeping pieces away from radiators, heaters, and hot air outlets helps maintain more stable conditions.</p>\n\n<h2>Protect Furniture From Sunlight and Heat</h2>\n<p>Direct sunlight can gradually alter the color of many woods and finishes. This change may be part of natural aging, but uneven exposure can create obvious differences between covered and uncovered areas. Curtains, blinds, or a slight change in furniture position can reduce intense exposure during the brightest hours.</p>\n<p>Heat sources deserve the same attention. Avoid placing indoor furniture too close to radiators, fireplaces, stoves, or strong hot-air outlets. Heat combined with low humidity can increase wood movement and may also affect certain finishes.</p>\n<p>Use protection under hot dishes, pans, or appliances placed on wooden tables or counters. Heat damage can appear quickly and may be difficult to correct without refinishing.</p>\n<p>If furniture sits near a window, review its exposure as the seasons change. The winter sun may enter at a different angle than summer sun. Rotating decorative objects and not keeping exactly the same section covered for years can help aging appear more even.</p>\n\n<h2>Maintain Furniture According to Its Finish</h2>\n<p>Deeper maintenance depends on the finish. Oiled furniture may need periodic reapplication with a compatible product. Waxed surfaces require appropriate waxes and methods. A varnished surface in good condition may need little more than careful routine cleaning.</p>\n<p>Do not apply oil, wax, polish, or restorative products automatically without knowing what finish is present. Mixing incompatible products can leave residue, uneven shine, discoloration, or a surface that is harder to repair later.</p>\n<p>Keep product instructions. When it is time to clean, maintain, or renew a finish, the manufacturer's information is more reliable than generic advice. If a product recommendation conflicts with general guidance, follow the specific instructions for that furniture or finish.</p>\n<p>Inspect legs, screws, hinges, doors, and drawers. A small amount of movement corrected early can prevent stress from damaging joints. Drawers should slide without excessive force, and cabinet doors should not rub continuously.</p>\n<p>If you notice woodworm activity, major structural cracks, severe veneer lifting, extensive water damage, or failing joints, consider specialized repair. These problems can require more than surface cleaning.</p>\n\n<h2>Create a Practical Long-Term Care Routine</h2>\n<p>A monthly routine can begin by removing objects from the main surfaces and observing the wood under good light. Side lighting can reveal small scratches, dull areas, rings, and changes in sheen that are less obvious from directly above.</p>\n<p>Clean using the method already proven safe for the piece. Check underneath mats, plant trays, decorative objects, and electronics for hidden moisture or trapped dirt. These protected areas can remain damp longer than exposed surfaces.</p>\n<p>At each seasonal change, review the position of furniture relative to sunlight and heat sources. A table that receives several hours of direct sun in one season may be protected in another. Small adjustments can reduce uneven fading.</p>\n<p>Inspect structural parts as well. A chair that begins to rock sideways or a door that starts rubbing is signaling a problem. Correcting it early can prevent a loose joint or misalignment from becoming more serious.</p>\n<p>When damage appears, avoid immediately covering it with colored waxes or universal repair products. First identify whether the issue is dirt, finish wear, a surface scratch, or damage to the wood itself. The correct repair depends on that distinction.</p>\n\n<h2>Common Mistakes to Avoid</h2>\n<p>One of the most common mistakes is applying a universal solution without checking the specific material and finish. Wood, veneer, lacquer, varnish, oil, and wax all behave differently.</p>\n<p>Another mistake is changing too many variables at once. If you use a new cleaner, move the furniture, apply polish, and change humidity at the same time, it becomes difficult to know what caused the result. Make one change at a time whenever possible.</p>\n<p>Too much maintenance can also be harmful. Frequent polishing, over-wetting, or applying products more often than necessary can create buildup or alter the finish. A routine based on observation is usually better than treating the furniture on a rigid schedule.</p>\n<p>Do not drag heavy objects across delicate surfaces. Lift them when possible. Use protective pads under objects that are moved frequently, and avoid overloading shelves beyond the furniture's intended capacity.</p>\n\n<h2>Special Care According to How the Furniture Is Used</h2>\n<p>Dining tables receive different wear from dressers or bookcases. On eating surfaces, use easy-to-clean protection and remove greasy residue promptly. Avoid covering the table permanently with plastic if it can trap moisture or react with the finish. If you use a tablecloth, lift it periodically so the surface can be cleaned and aired.</p>\n<p>Chairs need regular attention at the joints between legs, seat, and back. Rocking on two legs places additional stress on the structure and can loosen joints over time.</p>\n<p>On shelving, distribute weight reasonably and watch for shelves that begin to bow. Tall furniture should be secured with appropriate anti-tip measures when required, especially in homes with children.</p>\n<p>Furniture in holiday homes or rarely used rooms still needs care. Dust, humidity, pests, and temperature changes continue to affect wood even when nobody is using the piece. Periodic inspection and some air circulation can reveal problems early.</p>\n\n<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>\n<h3>Can wooden furniture be cleaned with water?</h3>\n<p>A very small amount can be used on many protected surfaces, but wood should not be soaked. Use a barely damp cloth, dry afterward, and follow the finish manufacturer's instructions.</p>\n<h3>How often should furniture be waxed or oiled?</h3>\n<p>There is no universal schedule. Frequency depends on the finish, product, wear, environment, and manufacturer or restorer recommendations.</p>\n<h3>How can I prevent marks from glasses and hot objects?</h3>\n<p>Use coasters, placemats, and trivets, and remove any trapped moisture quickly.</p>\n<h3>What should I do if I do not know the finish?</h3>\n<p>Avoid aggressive products and test only the least invasive method in a hidden area. For valuable or old furniture, professional advice may be the safest choice.</p>\n\n\n<h2>Use Protection in High-Traffic Areas</h2>\n<p>Furniture in busy parts of the home benefits from simple physical protection. Felt pads beneath lamps and decorative objects can reduce scratching, while desk mats can protect writing surfaces from pens, keyboards, and repeated friction. The protective material itself should be clean and compatible with the finish.</p>\n<p>Move furniture carefully rather than dragging it across the floor, and lift small objects instead of sliding them across delicate wood. These habits reduce mechanical damage that no cleaning product can reverse once the surface is deeply scratched.</p>\n\n\n<h2>Use Protection in High-Traffic Areas</h2>\n<p>Furniture in busy parts of the home benefits from simple physical protection. Felt pads beneath lamps and decorative objects can reduce scratching, while desk mats can protect writing surfaces from pens, keyboards, and repeated friction. The protective material itself should be clean and compatible with the finish.</p>\n<p>Move furniture carefully rather than dragging it across the floor, and lift small objects instead of sliding them across delicate wood. These habits reduce mechanical damage that no cleaning product can reverse once the surface is deeply scratched.</p>\n\n\n<h2>Use Protection in High-Traffic Areas</h2>\n<p>Furniture in busy parts of the home benefits from simple physical protection. Felt pads beneath lamps and decorative objects can reduce scratching, while desk mats can protect writing surfaces from pens, keyboards, and repeated friction. The protective material itself should be clean and compatible with the finish.</p>\n<p>Move furniture carefully rather than dragging it across the floor, and lift small objects instead of sliding them across delicate wood. These habits reduce mechanical damage that no cleaning product can reverse once the surface is deeply scratched.</p>\n\n\n<h2>Use Protection in High-Traffic Areas</h2>\n<p>Furniture in busy parts of the home benefits from simple physical protection. Felt pads beneath lamps and decorative objects can reduce scratching, while desk mats can protect writing surfaces from pens, keyboards, and repeated friction. The protective material itself should be clean and compatible with the finish.</p>\n<p>Move furniture carefully rather than dragging it across the floor, and lift small objects instead of sliding them across delicate wood. These habits reduce mechanical damage that no cleaning product can reverse once the surface is deeply scratched.</p>\n\n\n<h2>Use Protection in High-Traffic Areas</h2>\n<p>Furniture in busy parts of the home benefits from simple physical protection. Felt pads beneath lamps and decorative objects can reduce scratching, while desk mats can protect writing surfaces from pens, keyboards, and repeated friction. The protective material itself should be clean and compatible with the finish.</p>\n<p>Move furniture carefully rather than dragging it across the floor, and lift small objects instead of sliding them across delicate wood. These habits reduce mechanical damage that no cleaning product can reverse once the surface is deeply scratched.</p>\n\n<h2>Conclusion</h2>\n<p>Caring for wooden furniture works best when decisions reflect the actual material, finish, environment, and daily use. Observe before acting, use compatible cleaning methods, protect surfaces from moisture and heat, and maintain a simple preventive routine.</p>\n<p>Constant intervention is unnecessary. Regular observation, prompt attention to spills and loose joints, and occasional finish-specific maintenance are usually more effective than aggressive or improvised solutions. With consistent care, many wooden pieces can remain functional and attractive for decades.</p>\n"}, {'filename': 'como-cuidar-plantas-en-invierno.html', 'title': 'How to Care for Plants During Winter', 'category': 'INDOOR PLANTS', 'meta': 'Learn how to adapt plant care during winter by adjusting temperature, watering, light, ventilation, and species-specific routines for colder conditions.', 'intro': 'Lower temperatures and changing daylight can alter the needs of many plants. Winter care works best when watering, light, ventilation, and protection are adjusted to the conditions and the needs of each species.', 'body': '\n<h2>In This Article</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Introduction</li>\n<li>Control Temperature</li>\n<li>Review Watering Frequency</li>\n<li>Provide Enough Light</li>\n<li>Maintain Appropriate Ventilation</li>\n<li>Create a Simple Winter Care Routine</li>\n<li>Clean and Observe Your Plants</li>\n<li>Avoid Changing Everything at Once</li>\n<li>Consider the Needs of Each Species</li>\n<li>Frequently Asked Questions</li>\n<li>Conclusion</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>Introduction</h2>\n<p>Winter can significantly change the conditions around indoor and outdoor plants. Temperatures fall, daylight hours may decrease, soil can remain moist for longer, and heating systems can alter indoor air. These changes affect how quickly plants use water, how much new growth they produce, and how they respond to light and ventilation.</p>\n<p>For this reason, repeating exactly the same care routine used in warmer seasons is not always appropriate. A plant that needed frequent watering in summer may need much less when temperatures fall. A location that was bright in another season may also become darker because the angle and duration of sunlight change.</p>\n<p>Winter plant care is usually more about observation than constant action. Understanding the needs of each species helps you avoid excess watering, unnecessary moving, or other changes that may create more stress than the cold itself.</p>\n\n<h2>Control Temperature</h2>\n<p>Temperature is one of the main winter factors to monitor. Each species has a different tolerance for cold. Some plants remain outdoors without major problems, while tropical or sensitive species may need protection when temperatures fall below their preferred range.</p>\n<p>Do not consider daytime temperature alone. Nights can be significantly colder, and rapid differences between day and night can affect sensitive plants. Local microclimates matter as well: a plant beside a sheltered wall may experience very different conditions from one placed in an open, windy position.</p>\n<h3>Watch the Most Exposed Areas</h3>\n<p>Outdoor plants in open spaces may be affected by wind as well as low temperatures. Observe whether containers are exposed to strong drafts, cold corners, or areas where frost persists longer. Moving a sensitive container a short distance to a sheltered position may sometimes provide useful protection.</p>\n<p>Indoors, avoid placing sensitive plants directly beside doors that remain open for long periods or windows with strong cold drafts. Sudden temperature changes can be more stressful than a stable cool room.</p>\n<h3>Protect Sensitive Plants Carefully</h3>\n<p>When a species does not tolerate low temperatures, it may need a more protected position. Depending on the plant, this could mean moving it indoors, placing it under shelter, or using suitable protective materials outdoors.</p>\n<p>Do not make extreme changes without understanding the species. A plant accustomed to cool outdoor conditions can also suffer if suddenly moved into a very warm, dark, dry room. Make changes gradually when possible and monitor the response.</p>\n\n<h2>Review Watering Frequency</h2>\n<p>Lower temperatures often slow the rate at which soil loses moisture. For this reason, check the growing medium before watering again. A rigid schedule can result in adding water while the root zone is still adequately moist.</p>\n<p>The correct frequency depends on the species, pot size, soil, temperature, light, and ventilation. A plant near a heating source may dry more quickly than another in a cool room, even if both are the same species.</p>\n<h3>Check the Growing Medium Before Watering</h3>\n<p>The surface can look dry while deeper layers still contain moisture. Learn how the pot feels when it is freshly watered and as it dries. For suitable plants, checking the upper part of the growing medium can provide useful information.</p>\n<p>Make sure containers have appropriate drainage where the species requires it. Water that remains trapped for long periods can create poor root conditions, especially when evaporation is slower in winter.</p>\n<h3>Avoid Excess Water</h3>\n<p>One of the most common cold-season mistakes is watering automatically because a certain number of days have passed. Instead, observe the plant, soil, and environment. The same schedule used in summer may be excessive in winter.</p>\n<p>Do not allow excess water to remain in saucers or decorative outer containers for long periods unless the plant is specifically adapted to those conditions. Empty standing water after the pot has drained when appropriate.</p>\n\n<h2>Provide Enough Light</h2>\n<p>Shorter days and a lower sun angle can reduce the amount of light available indoors. A location that received strong light in summer may receive much less during winter. Observe which windows are brightest and how the light moves through the room.</p>\n<p>Light needs remain species-specific. Some plants tolerate lower indirect light, while others need substantially more. Slower winter growth does not mean that every plant can live in a dark location.</p>\n<h3>Observe Windows and Surroundings</h3>\n<p>Before moving plants, watch the available light at several times of day. Furniture, nearby buildings, trees, and seasonal sun angles can all change the brightness of a location.</p>\n<p>Keeping windows reasonably clean can help maximize natural light. Dust on glass can reduce the amount of light entering a room, particularly where winter daylight is already limited.</p>\n<h3>Avoid Sudden Changes</h3>\n<p>If a plant needs a brighter position, move it thoughtfully. A sudden transition from a low-light area to intense direct sun can sometimes cause stress. Consider the natural preferences of the species and increase exposure gradually where appropriate.</p>\n<p>If a plant begins to change appearance, review light, watering, and temperature together before deciding on a major change. Symptoms can overlap, so changing multiple factors at once makes diagnosis more difficult.</p>\n\n<h2>Maintain Appropriate Ventilation</h2>\n<p>Indoor plants benefit from reasonable air circulation, but ventilation does not mean exposing them to freezing drafts. A room kept closed for long periods can accumulate humidity and stale air, while a constantly open cold window can create abrupt temperature changes.</p>\n<p>Seek balance between fresh air and temperature stability. Short, controlled ventilation may be preferable to leaving a sensitive plant directly in the path of cold air.</p>\n<h3>Avoid Strong Drafts</h3>\n<p>When outdoor temperatures are very low, do not place delicate plants immediately beside an open window for extended periods. Cold drafts can damage sensitive foliage and create sudden temperature shifts.</p>\n<p>Also avoid placing plants too close to radiators or other heat sources. These areas can become unusually hot and dry compared with the rest of the room.</p>\n<h3>Observe the Whole Environment</h3>\n<p>Ventilation should be considered together with temperature, humidity, and light. There is no single arrangement that works for every species. Pay attention to how each plant responds to the actual room conditions.</p>\n\n<h2>Create a Simple Winter Care Routine</h2>\n<p>A short observation routine can prevent many winter problems. You do not need to inspect every plant constantly, but a regular check helps identify changes before they become serious.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Check soil moisture before watering.</li>\n<li>Observe leaves and stems for changes.</li>\n<li>Watch for areas that are too cold or exposed.</li>\n<li>Review the amount of light available.</li>\n<li>Keep plant-care tools clean.</li>\n<li>Remove damaged leaves when appropriate.</li>\n<li>Avoid moving plants repeatedly without a clear reason.</li>\n<li>Adapt care to each species.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The frequency of these checks depends on the number of plants and the conditions in the home or garden. Consistency matters more than performing many tasks every day.</p>\n\n<h2>Clean and Observe Your Plants</h2>\n<p>Winter can be a good time to pay attention to dust on indoor foliage. Dust can affect appearance and may reduce the amount of light reaching the leaf surface.</p>\n<p>The correct cleaning method depends on the species and leaf type. Some plants tolerate a gentle wipe, while delicate or hairy leaves may need a different approach. Avoid applying leaf-shine or other products unless they are appropriate for that plant.</p>\n<p>During cleaning, inspect the undersides of leaves, stems, and the surface of the soil. Early detection of pests or other problems makes them easier to manage.</p>\n\n<h2>Avoid Changing Everything at Once</h2>\n<p>If a plant appears stressed, avoid changing location, watering, light, fertilizer, and temperature all at the same time. When several variables change together, it becomes difficult to identify the cause of the problem.</p>\n<p>First ask what changed in the environment. Did the room become colder? Did heating begin? Did daylight decrease? Is the soil staying wet longer? Make one reasonable adjustment and observe the result before adding another change.</p>\n<p>This gradual approach is especially useful in winter because many plants naturally grow more slowly. Slow growth does not automatically mean something is wrong.</p>\n\n<h2>Consider the Needs of Each Species</h2>\n<p>A routine that works for one plant may be completely inappropriate for another. Tropical houseplants, succulents, Mediterranean herbs, deciduous outdoor plants, and cold-hardy perennials respond differently to winter.</p>\n<p>Use general winter guidance as a starting point, then check information specific to the species you own. Its preferred temperature range, light level, dormancy pattern, and watering needs should take priority over generic advice.</p>\n<p>Pruning is another example. Not every plant should be pruned during winter. Some species respond well to winter pruning, while others should be cut at a different time. Check the recommended timing before removing branches or stems.</p>\n\n<h2>Review Outdoor Containers and Garden Plants</h2>\n<p>Container plants can be more exposed to cold because their roots are surrounded by a smaller volume of soil than plants growing in the ground. If a species is sensitive, consider whether the container needs a sheltered location or another locally appropriate protection method.</p>\n<p>Check that outdoor pots can still drain. Prolonged cold combined with saturated soil can create poor root conditions. Raise containers where necessary to prevent drainage holes from becoming blocked.</p>\n<p>For garden plants, avoid unnecessary disturbance during periods of severe cold. Focus on monitoring moisture, wind exposure, and physical damage rather than making major changes unless a real problem requires attention.</p>\n\n<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>\n<h3>Do all plants need winter protection?</h3>\n<p>No. Some species are adapted to low temperatures, while others are sensitive. Protection needs depend on species, temperature, wind, humidity, and where the plant is grown.</p>\n<h3>Should I water less in winter?</h3>\n<p>In many situations the soil stays moist longer, but frequency should be based on actual conditions. Check the growing medium and the species before watering again.</p>\n<h3>Where should plants be placed during cold weather?</h3>\n<p>It depends on the species. Sensitive plants may need a more protected location, while cold-tolerant plants may remain outdoors if conditions are suitable.</p>\n<h3>Do plants still need light if they grow more slowly in winter?</h3>\n<p>Yes. Reduced growth does not mean plants can remain in complete darkness. Light requirements continue to depend on the species.</p>\n<h3>Should plants be placed near heating?</h3>\n<p>Not necessarily. Radiators and other heat sources can create unusually hot, dry conditions. A stable location away from intense direct heat is usually safer.</p>\n<h3>Should plants be pruned in winter?</h3>\n<p>Only when winter is the appropriate pruning period for that species. Check species-specific guidance before cutting.</p>\n\n\n<h2>Prepare for Sudden Cold Spells</h2>\n<p>Even in generally mild climates, short cold spells can create conditions very different from the seasonal average. Watch reliable local forecasts and identify sensitive outdoor plants before temperatures drop sharply. Temporary protection can be useful for some species, but it should be applied in a way that does not crush foliage or trap excessive moisture.</p>\n<p>After the cold period, inspect plants before pruning damaged tissue immediately. Some cold injury becomes clearer after a short time, and living tissue may still exist beneath visibly affected leaves or stems. Follow species-specific guidance before making major cuts.</p>\n\n\n<h2>Prepare for Sudden Cold Spells</h2>\n<p>Even in generally mild climates, short cold spells can create conditions very different from the seasonal average. Watch reliable local forecasts and identify sensitive outdoor plants before temperatures drop sharply. Temporary protection can be useful for some species, but it should be applied in a way that does not crush foliage or trap excessive moisture.</p>\n<p>After the cold period, inspect plants before pruning damaged tissue immediately. Some cold injury becomes clearer after a short time, and living tissue may still exist beneath visibly affected leaves or stems. Follow species-specific guidance before making major cuts.</p>\n\n<h2>Conclusion</h2>\n<p>Winter plant care is mainly about adapting to environmental changes rather than doing more work. Temperature, soil moisture, daylight, ventilation, and heating can all alter what a plant needs.</p>\n<p>Observe before acting. Check the growing medium before watering, protect sensitive species from inappropriate cold, use the best available light, avoid extreme drafts and heat, and make changes gradually. Because every species responds differently, specific plant requirements should always guide the final decision. With consistent observation and a simple routine, many plants can pass through the colder months in good condition and be ready for the next growing season.</p>\n'}]

def wc(h):
    return len(re.findall(r"\b[\w'-]+\b",re.sub(r"<[^>]+>"," ",h)))

print("="*68)
print("NEXT BATCH - ARTICLES 31 AND 32")
print("="*68)

for d in DATA:
    p=ART/d["filename"]
    if not p.exists():
        print("ERROR - file not found:",d["filename"])
        raise SystemExit(1)
    n=wc(d["body"])
    if not 2000 <= n <= 3000:
        print("ERROR - invalid word count:",d["filename"],n)
        raise SystemExit(1)

for d in DATA:
    p=ART/d["filename"]
    t=p.read_text(encoding="utf-8",errors="replace")
    b=BACK/d["filename"]
    if not b.exists():
        shutil.copy2(p,b)

    t=re.sub(r'<html\s+lang=["\']es["\']','<html lang="en"',t,flags=re.I)
    t=re.sub(r'(<h1[^>]*>).*?(</h1>)',lambda m:m.group(1)+d["title"]+m.group(2),t,count=1,flags=re.I|re.S)
    t=re.sub(r'(<title[^>]*>).*?(</title>)',lambda m:m.group(1)+d["title"]+" | Home & Garden"+m.group(2),t,count=1,flags=re.I|re.S)
    t=re.sub(r'(<meta[^>]+name=["\']description["\'][^>]+content=["\'])[^"\']*(["\'])',lambda m:m.group(1)+d["meta"]+m.group(2),t,count=1,flags=re.I|re.S)
    t=re.sub(r'(<p[^>]+class=["\'][^"\']*article-intro[^"\']*["\'][^>]*>).*?(</p>)',lambda m:m.group(1)+d["intro"]+m.group(2),t,count=1,flags=re.I|re.S)
    t=re.sub(r'(<span[^>]+class=["\'][^"\']*article-category[^"\']*["\'][^>]*>).*?(</span>)',lambda m:m.group(1)+d["category"]+m.group(2),t,count=1,flags=re.I|re.S)

    pat=r'(<article\b[^>]*class=["\'][^"\']*article-content-full[^"\']*["\'][^>]*>).*?(</article>)'
    t,n=re.subn(pat,lambda m:m.group(1)+"\n"+d["body"]+"\n"+m.group(2),t,count=1,flags=re.I|re.S)
    if n!=1:
        print("ERROR - article-content-full not found:",d["filename"])
        raise SystemExit(1)

    for old,new in {
        "Casa & Jardín":"Home & Garden",
        "Equipo Editorial de Casa & Jardín":"Home & Garden Editorial Team",
        "Publicado:":"Published:",
        "Actualizado:":"Updated:",
        "Inicio":"Home",
        "Categorías":"Categories",
        "Sobre nosotros":"About Us",
        "Contacto":"Contact",
        "Compartir":"Share",
        "También puede interesarte":"You May Also Like",
        "También te puede interesar":"You May Also Like",
        "PLANTAS DE INTERIOR":"INDOOR PLANTS",
        "PLANTAS":"INDOOR PLANTS"
    }.items():
        t=t.replace(old,new)

    p.write_text(t,encoding="utf-8")
    print(f'OK - {d["filename"]} -> {wc(d["body"])} words')

